1294	\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}
1295	\label{sec:apply-ldt}
1296	
1297	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
1298	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
1299	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
1300	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
1301	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
1302	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
1303	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
1304	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
1305	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply. The seed-bearing source route is
1306	blocked at the instantiation step for admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$.
1307	The global-pair conclusion also has the directly indexed proof below. The
1308	obstruction is recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and
1309	analyzed, together with candidate repairs, in
1310	\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
